      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© Veterinary Emergency and Critical Care Society 2017.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To describe the use of single pass lipid dialysis (SPLD) for treatment of ivermectin toxicosis in 2 Australian Shepherd dogs with the ABCB1-1Δ gene mutation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
            "Label": "CASE SERIES SUMMARY",
            "content": "Two Australian Shepherd dogs were presented for treatment of ivermectin toxicosis. Dogs were initially treated with intravenous lipid emulsion and supportive care, without improvement of clinical signs. They both developed respiratory paralysis and required mechanical ventilation. In order to increase the clearance of circulating ivermectin, SPLD was performed using dialysate containing 5% lipid. Blood samples were obtained immediately before and after dialysis and analyzed for serum ivermectin concentration. Ivermectin reduction ratio was calculated at 29% and 39% for each dog, respectively. When compared to intrinsic total body ivermectin clearance, only the second dog had a relative improvement of plasma clearance following SPLD. Both dogs were confirmed to be homozygous for ABCB1-1Δ gene mutations. Both dogs remained ventilator dependent for several days and ultimately made a full recovery."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "NEW OR UNIQUE INFORMATION PROVIDED",
            "content": "SPLD may be an adjunctive detoxification strategy for highly lipophilic toxins such as ivermectin."
          }
        ]
      },
